## Production posture

Set `APP_RUNTIME_MODE=production` in the process environment — systemd
`Environment=`/`EnvironmentFile=`, `docker --env-file`, or platform secrets;
production never reads `.env`, so every variable it carried moves there too.
In production this host refuses to boot when:

- `APP_RUNTIME_MODE` is unset or not exactly `development` or `production`;
- `PUBLIC_BASE_URL` is unset, not `https`, or not a bare origin
  (`web.ServerConfig.ValidateProduction`);
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}
- a database target is not verified TLS — `sslmode=verify-full` (or
  `verify-ca` with `sslrootcert`); a unix socket counts as local — or query
  logging is on (`pgxdb.ErrTLSRequired`, `pgxdb.ErrQueryLoggingRefused`);
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .DB "turso"}}
- the Turso URL is not TLS (`libsql://`, `https://`, or `wss://` without
  `tls=0`; a `file:` database is local) (`turso.ErrTLSRequired`);
{{- end}}
- a pocket you mount meets one of its own development-only options: pass
  every pocket this same `mode`, and write any waiver as a literal
  acknowledgement beside the thing it waives.

TLS terminates at your edge (load balancer, ingress, platform router); the app
sends HSTS and the browser defaults through `web.SecurityHeaders()`, and
`TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` names the proxies in front of it so `web.TrustProxies`
resolves the real client IP.

Check the composition before every deploy. This scaffold pins no tool, so CI
runs the guard directly:

```
go run github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/workshop/gopernicus@v0.6.0 guard --posture .
```

It exits 0 clean and 1 with findings, and prints one `ack` line per waiver you
wrote. Exit 2 is a broken invocation — a release older than v0.5.0 does not
know `--posture` — so CI treats it as a failure, never a pass.
{{- if eq .DB "pgx"}}

### Audit tables and the two database roles

The pgx stores with an audit table (authentication `security_events`,
authorization `iam_audit` and `iam_operations`, secrets `secrets_audit`) need
two database roles, created with SQL rather than a provider console (console
roles can carry `pg_write_all_data`):

- a **migrator** that owns the schema, the tables, their sequences and the
  trigger functions, and runs `make migrate`;
- a **runtime** role the server connects as. On the audit tables it gets
  `SELECT` and `INSERT`, plus `USAGE` on their sequences; `UPDATE`, `DELETE`,
  `TRUNCATE` and `TRIGGER` are revoked — except that `DELETE` stays granted on
  `iam_operations`, whose trigger decides which rows may go.

Each store enforces this when it is constructed. The contract, in one
sentence: **in production a store refuses to boot when its runtime role is
superuser, owns an audit table, can UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE/TRIGGER it without
the verified triggers, or cannot insert into it.** The immutability trigger
files are migrations — delete them from your ledger before applying if you
decline them.

Before every deploy, list each mounted pgx store's `AuditTables()` in
`postureTables` (`workshop/migrations/main.go`), run the preflight as the
migrator against the runtime role, and archive its output with the release:

```
APP_RUNTIME_MODE=production go run ./workshop/migrations -posture-role <runtime role>
```

It prints one line per table —
`table=<t> protected_by=<privilege|trigger|waiver|none> owner=… insert=… sequence_usage=… trigger=<state>`
— and exits non-zero when any table is unprotected or cannot be appended to.
{{- end}}
